#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""🔴 WHICH WINDOWS HAVE BEEN WAITING ON THE OPERATOR TOO LONG — oldest first.

WHY THIS EXISTS — THE MEASUREMENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
`session-manager` computes `waiting_probable` and NOTHING in this repo consumes
it. Measured: two windows sat unanswered for 54.5h and 29.6h while the operator
answered ~98 other prompts in the same 22 hours. That is not an attention
problem — the operator was answering continuously. It is a SCHEDULING problem:
nothing surfaces the waiting set oldest-first, so the two oldest were the two
least likely to be seen.

This tool adds exactly TWO things to `session-manager`: a CLOCK and a THRESHOLD.

🔴 IT DOES NOT RE-DERIVE THE WAITING PREDICATE, AND THAT IS THE POINT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
`waiting_probable` has a real false-positive history — a pane showing ANOTHER
session's output tripping `trailing_question`, `context_exhausted` firing on a
busy window. Every one of those bugs lives in the detector, and a second copy of
the detector regenerates every one of them (RULES.md: "one rule, one place — a
predicate duplicated across call sites regenerates the same bug at every site").
So this file contains NO pane scraping, NO regex over captured text, and NO
notion of what a question looks like. It consumes `waiting_probable`,
`waiting_signals` and `waiting_status` as given.

🔴 THE CLOCK — WHY `age_secs` IS NOT THE ANSWER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
`age_secs` is time since the window's last LEDGER HEARTBEAT, not time since it
asked. Measured: a row was flagged waiting while `busy` with an `age_secs` of
2.5 SECONDS — its question was not 2.5s old. Read as "how long has this been
waiting", `age_secs` is a number from a different question that happens to be in
the same units, which is the most dangerous shape a wrong number can have.

So this tool keeps its own clock. The FIRST time it observes a window in a
waiting state it stamps `first_seen`; the threshold runs off that stamp. Each
row publishes `waited_source` naming which term produced its number:

    first_seen           — elapsed since our own stamp. A strict lower bound on
                           the true wait, exact once the window has been
                           observed across the whole wait.
    ledger_age_fallback  — the ledger age observed AT stamp time. Used on the
                           first observation (elapsed is 0 then and would report
                           a 54h wait as "0s"), and thereafter whenever it is
                           still the LARGER of the two.
    unmeasured           — neither term is available. NEVER coerced to 0.

🔴 WHY `max(elapsed, age_at_first_seen)` AND NOT SIMPLY THE STAMP.
The naive rule — "stamp on first sight, then report elapsed" — has a hole that
defeats the whole tool. A window already 54h into its wait when this tool first
runs gets `first_seen = now`; ten minutes later it reports 600s and drops below
the threshold it was ABOVE on the first run. The oldest window in the fleet
would be surfaced once and then hidden, which is the exact failure mode this
tool exists to remove. So the stamp records the ledger age observed at stamp
time and the reported wait is the larger of the two terms. Each term's weakness
is the other's strength: elapsed starts at 0 and only grows honestly; the ledger
age can be wildly LOW (the 2.5s row) but is a good estimate when the last
recorded turn IS the turn that asked. Taking the max never regresses, and
`waited_source` says which term won, so no number is unlabelled.

🔴 THE KEY IS `(host, tmux server pid, window_id)` — NOT `session:index`
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Window INDEXES renumber when windows close, so a stamp keyed on `scratch2:3`
silently transfers to whatever window slides into slot 3. `window_id` (`@41`) is
stable for the life of a window — but tmux restarts them at `@0` when the SERVER
restarts, so `@41` after a restart is a different window with the same name.
`scripts/lib/agent_ledger.py` already solved this and its solution is reused
rather than re-derived: `#{pid}` is the tmux SERVER pid, constant across every
window of a server and different after a restart, so it is an exact generation
check. `session-manager` already reads it per host and publishes it at
`report["ledger"]["hosts"][<host>]["tmux_pid"]`.

When that pid is absent (a `--no-ledger` report, a host whose ledger read
failed) the generation CANNOT be checked. This tool then degrades LOUDLY: the
stamps are kept under the sentinel generation, every affected row carries
`generation_checked: false`, and `coverage.generation_unchecked_hosts` names the
hosts, so a consumer can never read an unchecked stamp as a checked one.

🔴 THE STATE FILE IS PRUNED, EVERY RUN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
fuzzyclaw reached 401 files, ~90% stale, because nothing ever pruned. A stamp
here is dropped when its window is no longer waiting (the RESET that stops an
answered-then-re-asked window inheriting an ancient age), when its window no
longer exists, and when its generation no longer matches the live server.

🔴 A STAMP IS NEVER PRUNED ON A HOST WE DID NOT OBSERVE. An unreachable host
produces no rows, and "no rows" is indistinguishable from "no windows" only if
you forget which question you asked. Pruning there would destroy exactly the
long-lived stamps this tool exists to accumulate, and it would do it silently.
Those stamps are KEPT and counted as `kept_unobserved_host`.

🔴 NULL IS NOT ZERO
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
An unreachable host, an unmeasured capture batch or a row whose `waiting_status`
is not `ok` means that scope was NOT LOOKED AT. Its count is `null`, never `0`.
A reassuring zero from a scan that walked nothing is the failure mode, not the
all-clear. `per_host[<host>].over_threshold` is `null` for an unmeasured host,
the global total is `null` when NO host was measured, and `counts_are_partial`
is true whenever any scope went unmeasured.

🔴 WHAT THE `idle_no_signal` BUCKET CANNOT TELL YOU — IN THE OUTPUT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
`session-manager` does not read PRs. A window idle because its PR is blocked
looks EXACTLY like a window idle because the work is done. Both land in
`idle_no_signal`. That sentence is published in the JSON (`caveats`) and printed
in the human view, because a report that presented finished work as needing
attention would burn the operator's trust in the three precise buckets beside it.

    run:  scripts/waiting-windows
          scripts/waiting-windows --json
          scripts/waiting-windows --json-file - < report.json
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# THE THRESHOLD
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 🔴 4 HOURS, AND THE NUMBER IS DERIVED FROM THE MEASUREMENT, NOT PICKED.
#
# The observed distribution has two clean modes:
#
#   * the ANSWERED mode — ~98 prompts answered in 22 hours, i.e. a prompt every
#     ~13.5 minutes on average. Sub-hour questions were being answered
#     continuously and need no tool at all; anything that fires on them is
#     noise, and a roll-up that cries wolf during an active working session is
#     one the operator stops reading.
#   * the STRANDED mode — 54.5h and 29.6h. Two windows, both far outside any
#     plausible working gap.
#
# There is nothing in between, so the threshold only has to land in the empty
# band. 4h sits ~18x above the observed answering cadence — comfortably past a
# meal, an errand, a long meeting or a school run, none of which are a
# scheduling failure — and ~7.4x below the SMALLER of the two outliers, so both
# are caught with a wide margin on either side. A 1h threshold would fire during
# ordinary work; a 24h threshold would have caught only one of the two.
#
# It is not a constant of nature. `--threshold-secs` and the
# `WAITING_WINDOWS_THRESHOLD` environment variable override it, and the report
# publishes `threshold_source` so a consumer can tell a tuned run from a default
# one.
#
# 🔴 NEITHER NAME USES THE RESERVED SUFFIX, deliberately.
# `test_clawgate_predicate_single_source.py` treats an assignment to any name
# ending in that suffix, outside `scripts/lib/clawgate_tasks.py`, as a
# re-spelling of clawgate's stuck-threshold constant — so it is reserved. This
# threshold is a different quantity measured on a different population — how
# long a tmux window has been waiting on the operator, not how long a clawgate
# task has been stuck — and the repo already keeps unrelated thresholds off the
# reserved suffix (`session-manager`'s `DEFAULT_STALE_THRESHOLD`). Renaming to
# match that convention is the fix; adding this file to the guard's exemption
# list would have been the workaround.
DEFAULT_WAIT_THRESHOLD = 4 * 3600
THRESHOLD_ENV = "WAITING_WINDOWS_THRESHOLD"

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# THE KIND VOCABULARY
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 🔴 CLASSIFY, DO NOT LUMP. The operator chose the WIDE population, and the four
# buckets need four different actions:
#
#   context_exhausted  — the window cannot proceed AT ALL without a human
#                        (`/compact`, or a fresh session). Hardest block.
#   selection_menu     — a modal is up. One keystroke unblocks it, but nothing
#                        else in that window can run until it is answered.
#   trailing_question  — the agent asked and stopped. It is blocked, but the
#                        work it did is intact and re-readable.
#   idle_no_signal     — no signal at all. See IDLE_NO_SIGNAL_CAVEAT: this
#                        bucket is the one that cannot distinguish "blocked" from
#                        "finished", and it must never be summed with the three
#                        above into one undifferentiated number.
#
# A single undifferentiated count would tell the operator to walk to a terminal
# without telling them what for, and would mix finished work into the total.
IDLE_NO_SIGNAL = "idle_no_signal"

# 🔴 PRECEDENCE, hardest block first. A row can carry several signals; `kind` is
# the single most-blocking one so the report sorts and buckets deterministically,
# and `kinds_all` carries the rest so the collapse is never lossy.
#
# 🔴 This tuple is pinned AGAINST `session-manager`'s own `WAITING_SIGNALS` by a
# seam test that fails when that set GROWS OR SHRINKS. A signal added there and
# not here would fall through to `unclassified_signals` and be reported as a
# nameless block; the ledger makes that arrive as a red test instead.
SIGNAL_PRECEDENCE = ("context_exhausted", "selection_menu", "trailing_question")

# Every kind this tool can emit, so a per-kind roll-up publishes the whole
# vocabulary rather than only the kinds that happened to occur. A bucket that is
# absent from the output because it did not occur, and a bucket that is absent
# because the code forgot it, look identical otherwise.
ALL_KINDS = SIGNAL_PRECEDENCE + (IDLE_NO_SIGNAL,)

# 🔴 The limit, stated in the OUTPUT rather than only in this docstring. A
# consumer that renders the JSON and never reads this file still gets the
# sentence. Pinned as a WHOLE STRING by a test: a guard on a couple of words
# would be walkable by rewording it into something that no longer warns.
IDLE_NO_SIGNAL_CAVEAT = (
    "session-manager does not read PRs, so a window idle because its PR is "
    "blocked looks exactly like a window idle because the work is done. The "
    "idle_no_signal bucket carries both and cannot separate them. Do not read "
    "it as a queue of work needing attention."
)

CLOCK_CAVEAT = (
    "waited_secs is this tool's own clock, not session-manager's age_secs. "
    "age_secs measures time since the window's last ledger heartbeat, which is "
    "a different question in the same units: a row was measured waiting while "
    "busy with an age_secs of 2.5 seconds. Read waited_source on every row."
)

GENERATION_CAVEAT = (
    "Stamps are keyed on (host, tmux server pid, window_id). tmux restarts "
    "window ids at @0 when the server restarts, so a stamp whose generation "
    "cannot be checked may belong to a window that no longer exists. Rows with "
    "generation_checked false are named in coverage.generation_unchecked_hosts."
)

# The sentinel generation for a host whose tmux server pid was not measured. It
# is a value the real signal (a pid, always digits) cannot produce, so an
# unchecked stamp can never collide with a checked one.
GENERATION_UNKNOWN = "gen-unknown"

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# THE SOURCE
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# 🔴 NOT `--lean`. The lean view is the agent-shaped one and it DROPS
# `window_id` (measured: `lean_row_fields` has `window_index` and no
# `window_id`), which is the only stable key a stamp can use. `--no-ch` because
# ClickHouse answers a question this tool never asks. NOT `--claude-only`,
# because the full window census is what tells a prune "this window still
# exists" — a row filtered out of the report is indistinguishable from a window
# that closed, and the agent filter is applied here instead, on `row["claude"]`.
SESSION_MANAGER_ARGV = ("--json", "--no-ch")

STATE_DIR_NAME = "waiting-windows"
STATE_FILE_NAME = "state.json"
STATE_VERSION = 1


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# State file location + I/O. $HOME is read at CALL time, never at import time,
# so a test can point it somewhere safe and so a long-lived process cannot bake
# in a stale home.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def state_dir() -> str:
    return os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".cache", STATE_DIR_NAME)


def state_path() -> str:
    return os.path.join(state_dir(), STATE_FILE_NAME)


def load_state(path):
    """-> {"stamps": {...}, "status": "ok"|"missing"|"malformed"|"error"}.

    🔴 A malformed or unreadable state file degrades to an EMPTY stamp set with
    the reason published, never to a crash and never silently. An empty set
    means every row falls back to `ledger_age_fallback` this run, which is
    correct and labelled — whereas a silent empty set would report the fleet's
    oldest waits as brand new.
    """
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            raw = json.load(fh)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return {"stamps": {}, "status": "missing"}
    except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        return {"stamps": {}, "status": "malformed"}
    except OSError:
        return {"stamps": {}, "status": "error"}
    if not isinstance(raw, dict) or not isinstance(raw.get("stamps"), dict):
        return {"stamps": {}, "status": "malformed"}
    stamps = {}
    for key, val in raw["stamps"].items():
        # A stamp missing `first_seen`, or carrying a non-numeric one, is not a
        # clock — dropping it costs one run of `ledger_age_fallback`, keeping it
        # would produce a wait computed from garbage.
        if isinstance(val, dict) and isinstance(val.get("first_seen"), (int, float)):
            stamps[key] = val
    return {"stamps": stamps, "status": "ok"}


def save_state(path, stamps) -> None:
    """Atomic replace. The temp file is created in the state dir (same
    filesystem, so `os.replace` is atomic) and is ALWAYS gone afterwards —
    on success by the rename, on failure by the unlink — so it can never be
    left behind to be read as a state file or counted as an artifact."""
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
    fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=os.path.dirname(path), prefix=".state-", suffix=".tmp")
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            json.dump({"version": STATE_VERSION, "stamps": stamps}, fh,
                      indent=1, sort_keys=True)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except BaseException:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass
        raise


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Pure classification
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def stamp_key(host, generation, window_id) -> str:
    """`host|generation|window_id`. `|` cannot occur in any of the three: hosts
    are `session-manager`'s own host names, a generation is digits or the
    sentinel, and a tmux window id is `@<n>`."""
    return "%s|%s|%s" % (host, generation, window_id)


def row_signals(row):
    """The signal NAMES on a row, deduplicated, in the row's own order."""
    out = []
    for sig in (row.get("waiting_signals") or ()):
        if not isinstance(sig, dict):
            continue
        name = sig.get("signal")
        if name and name not in out:
            out.append(name)
    return out


# The three answers a row can get. 🔴 `UNMEASURED` is a THIRD state, not a
# flavour of "not waiting": a row whose `waiting_status` is not `ok` is neither
# waiting nor not-waiting, and collapsing it into either one turns "we did not
# look" into a measured observation.
TRIAGE_WAITING = "waiting"
TRIAGE_NOT_IN_POPULATION = "not_in_population"
TRIAGE_UNMEASURED = "unmeasured"


def classify(row):
    """-> (state, kind, kinds_all, unclassified).

    🔴 THIS IS THE ONLY PLACE THE POPULATION IS DECIDED. An earlier revision
    also tested `waiting_status != "ok"` in `reconcile`, one line before calling
    this function — and a mutation sweep caught it: deleting the check HERE
    changed nothing, because the copy in the caller always won, so the guard was
    UNREACHABLE and its coverage was an illusion. One rule, one place; `reconcile`
    now asks this function and branches on the state it returns.
    """
    if not row.get("claude"):
        return TRIAGE_NOT_IN_POPULATION, None, [], []
    if row.get("waiting_status") != "ok":
        return TRIAGE_UNMEASURED, None, [], []
    if row.get("busy"):
        # A busy window is working, not waiting. This is also the row shape that
        # made `age_secs` untrustworthy (measured waiting while busy at 2.5s).
        return TRIAGE_NOT_IN_POPULATION, None, [], []
    if not row.get("waiting_probable"):
        return TRIAGE_WAITING, IDLE_NO_SIGNAL, [IDLE_NO_SIGNAL], []
    names = row_signals(row)
    known = [n for n in SIGNAL_PRECEDENCE if n in names]
    unclassified = sorted(n for n in names if n not in SIGNAL_PRECEDENCE)
    if known:
        return TRIAGE_WAITING, known[0], known + unclassified, unclassified
    if unclassified:
        # `waiting_probable` is true on a signal this build does not know. Say
        # so under its own name rather than filing it as one of ours.
        return TRIAGE_WAITING, unclassified[0], unclassified, unclassified
    # Probable with no signals at all: still a block, but an unnamed one.
    return TRIAGE_WAITING, "waiting_unclassified", ["waiting_unclassified"], []


def host_generations(report):
    """-> {host: generation}. `GENERATION_UNKNOWN` for any host whose tmux
    server pid was not measured, which is the loud-degradation path."""
    ledger_hosts = ((report.get("ledger") or {}).get("hosts") or {})
    out = {}
    for host in (report.get("hosts") or {}):
        pid = (ledger_hosts.get(host) or {}).get("tmux_pid")
        out[host] = str(pid) if pid else GENERATION_UNKNOWN
    return out


def host_observed(report):
    """-> {host: bool}. A host is OBSERVED when it was reachable AND its window
    list was actually measured. Both, because `list-panes` succeeding says
    nothing about `list-windows`, and a host that answered but returned no
    window list gives us no liveness facts to prune against."""
    out = {}
    for host, blk in (report.get("hosts") or {}).items():
        blk = blk or {}
        out[host] = bool(blk.get("reachable")) and bool(blk.get("windows_measured"))
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# THE CORE — pure. Takes a report, a stamp set and a clock; returns the report
# and the next stamp set. Touches no disk, no network and no tmux, which is what
# makes every test of it hermetic.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def reconcile(report, stamps, now, threshold_secs):
    generations = host_generations(report)
    observed = host_observed(report)
    hosts_blk = report.get("hosts") or {}

    live_ids = {h: set() for h in hosts_blk}
    rows_by_host = {h: [] for h in hosts_blk}
    for host, blk in hosts_blk.items():
        for row in ((blk or {}).get("windows") or []):
            wid = row.get("window_id")
            if wid:
                live_ids[host].add(wid)
            rows_by_host[host].append(row)

    next_stamps = {}
    out_rows = []
    unmeasured_rows = 0
    unmeasured_reasons = {}
    rows_without_window_id = 0

    for host, rows in rows_by_host.items():
        gen = generations.get(host, GENERATION_UNKNOWN)
        for row in rows:
            state, kind, kinds_all, unclassified = classify(row)
            if state == TRIAGE_UNMEASURED:
                unmeasured_rows += 1
                reason = row.get("waiting_status") or "none"
                unmeasured_reasons[reason] = unmeasured_reasons.get(reason, 0) + 1
                continue
            if state != TRIAGE_WAITING:
                continue
            wid = row.get("window_id")
            if not wid:
                # 🔴 No stable key -> no clock. Counted and published rather than
                # dropped, because a report that silently omits rows it could not
                # key would under-count the very population it exists to surface.
                rows_without_window_id += 1
                continue
            key = stamp_key(host, gen, wid)
            age = row.get("age_secs")
            age = float(age) if isinstance(age, (int, float)) else None
            prior = stamps.get(key)
            if prior is None:
                first_seen = float(now)
                age_at_first_seen = age
            else:
                first_seen = float(prior["first_seen"])
                stored = prior.get("age_at_first_seen")
                age_at_first_seen = float(stored) if isinstance(stored, (int, float)) else None
            next_stamps[key] = {
                "first_seen": first_seen,
                "age_at_first_seen": age_at_first_seen,
                "host": host,
                "window_id": wid,
                "generation": gen,
                "kind": kind,
                "last_seen": float(now),
            }

            elapsed = float(now) - first_seen
            # 🔴 max(), and `waited_source` names the term that WON. See the
            # module docstring for why the stamp alone regresses a 54h window to
            # 600s on the second run.
            if age_at_first_seen is not None and age_at_first_seen > elapsed:
                waited, source = age_at_first_seen, "ledger_age_fallback"
            elif prior is not None or age_at_first_seen is not None:
                waited, source = elapsed, "first_seen"
            else:
                # First observation AND no ledger age: elapsed is 0, which would
                # be a measured-looking zero for a window we have never timed.
                waited, source = None, "unmeasured"

            out_rows.append({
                "host": host,
                "session": row.get("session"),
                "window_index": row.get("window_index"),
                "window_id": wid,
                "label": row.get("label"),
                "hotkey": row.get("hotkey"),
                "path": row.get("path"),
                "task": row.get("task"),
                "claude_session_id": row.get("claude_session_id"),
                "status": row.get("status"),
                "kind": kind,
                "kinds_all": kinds_all,
                "unclassified_signals": unclassified,
                "waited_secs": waited,
                "waited_source": source,
                "over_threshold": (waited is not None and waited > threshold_secs),
                "age_secs": age,
                "age_source": row.get("age_source"),
                "generation": gen,
                "generation_checked": gen != GENERATION_UNKNOWN,
                "unsent_prompt_status": row.get("unsent_prompt_status"),
                "has_unsent_prompt": bool(row.get("unsent_prompt")),
            })

    prune = prune_stamps(stamps, next_stamps, observed, generations, live_ids)
    next_stamps.update(prune["kept"])

    # Oldest first — the whole reason this tool exists. `None` waits sort LAST:
    # an unmeasured wait is not an old one, and putting it at the top would push
    # a measured 54h row below a row we could not time at all.
    out_rows.sort(key=lambda r: (r["waited_secs"] is None,
                                 -(r["waited_secs"] or 0.0),
                                 r["host"], r["window_id"]))

    coverage = build_coverage(report, observed, generations,
                              unmeasured_rows, unmeasured_reasons,
                              rows_without_window_id)
    counts = build_counts(out_rows, observed, coverage)
    return {
        "rows": out_rows,
        "counts": counts,
        "coverage": coverage,
        "prune": prune["counts"],
    }, next_stamps


def prune_stamps(stamps, next_stamps, observed, generations, live_ids):
    """🔴 THE PRUNE. fuzzyclaw reached 401 files, ~90% stale, because nothing
    ever pruned. Four distinct outcomes, each counted under its own name so the
    report can say WHY a stamp went rather than only that the file shrank:

      kept_unobserved_host  — the host was not observed this run. NOT pruned:
                              absence of rows from an unreachable host is not
                              evidence the windows are gone, and pruning there
                              would silently destroy the oldest stamps we have.
      reset_not_waiting     — the window exists and is no longer waiting. This
                              is the RESET: an answered-then-re-asked window
                              must not inherit its previous wait.
      pruned_window_gone    — the window id is not in the live set.
      pruned_generation     — the stamp's generation is not the host's live one,
                              i.e. the tmux server restarted. Those window ids
                              have been reissued to different windows.
    """
    counts = {"kept_unobserved_host": 0, "reset_not_waiting": 0,
              "pruned_window_gone": 0, "pruned_generation": 0,
              "kept_generation_unknown": 0}
    kept = {}
    for key, stamp in stamps.items():
        if key in next_stamps:
            continue
        host = stamp.get("host")
        if not observed.get(host):
            counts["kept_unobserved_host"] += 1
            kept[key] = stamp
            continue
        live_gen = generations.get(host, GENERATION_UNKNOWN)
        if live_gen == GENERATION_UNKNOWN:
            # 🔴 Cannot check the generation, so cannot justify dropping it as a
            # different generation's window. Kept, and counted under its own
            # name so "we kept it" is never read as "we verified it".
            counts["kept_generation_unknown"] += 1
            kept[key] = stamp
            continue
        if stamp.get("generation") != live_gen:
            counts["pruned_generation"] += 1
            continue
        if stamp.get("window_id") in live_ids.get(host, set()):
            counts["reset_not_waiting"] += 1
            continue
        counts["pruned_window_gone"] += 1
    return {"kept": kept, "counts": counts}


def build_coverage(report, observed, generations,
                   unmeasured_rows, unmeasured_reasons, rows_without_window_id):
    """🔴 NULL IS NOT ZERO — everything this run could NOT see, propagated."""
    summary = report.get("summary") or {}
    hosts_blk = report.get("hosts") or {}
    per_host = {}
    for host, blk in hosts_blk.items():
        blk = blk or {}
        per_host[host] = {
            "observed": bool(observed.get(host)),
            "reachable": blk.get("reachable"),
            "windows_measured": blk.get("windows_measured"),
            "captures_measured": blk.get("captures_measured"),
            "captures_status": blk.get("captures_status"),
            "error": blk.get("error") or blk.get("windows_error"),
            "generation": generations.get(host),
            "generation_checked": generations.get(host) != GENERATION_UNKNOWN,
        }
    unobserved = sorted(h for h, ok in observed.items() if not ok)
    gen_unchecked = sorted(h for h, g in generations.items()
                           if g == GENERATION_UNKNOWN)
    captures_unmeasured = sorted(
        h for h, blk in hosts_blk.items()
        if (blk or {}).get("captures_measured") is not True)
    return {
        "per_host": per_host,
        "hosts_unobserved": unobserved,
        "hosts_unreachable": list(summary.get("hosts_unreachable") or []),
        "hosts_captures_unmeasured": captures_unmeasured,
        "generation_unchecked_hosts": gen_unchecked,
        "windows_unmeasured": list(summary.get("windows_unmeasured") or []),
        "rows_unmeasured": unmeasured_rows,
        "rows_unmeasured_reasons": unmeasured_reasons,
        "rows_without_window_id": rows_without_window_id,
        # 🔴 The single boolean a consumer must branch on before believing a
        # zero. A field that merely EXISTS is not a guard; this one is read by
        # `build_counts` and by the human renderer, and both are tested.
        "complete": (not unobserved and not gen_unchecked
                     and not captures_unmeasured
                     and unmeasured_rows == 0 and rows_without_window_id == 0
                     and not (summary.get("windows_unmeasured") or [])),
    }


def build_counts(rows, observed, coverage):
    """Per-kind and per-host counts. 🔴 `null`, never `0`, for any scope that
    was not measured — and `null` for the TOTAL when NO host was observed at
    all, because "0 windows are overdue" from a scan that walked nothing is the
    one sentence this tool must never emit."""
    any_observed = any(observed.values())
    over = [r for r in rows if r["over_threshold"]]

    by_kind = {k: 0 for k in ALL_KINDS}
    pop_by_kind = {k: 0 for k in ALL_KINDS}
    for r in rows:
        pop_by_kind[r["kind"]] = pop_by_kind.get(r["kind"], 0) + 1
    for r in over:
        by_kind[r["kind"]] = by_kind.get(r["kind"], 0) + 1

    per_host = {}
    for host, ok in observed.items():
        if not ok:
            per_host[host] = {"over_threshold": None, "waiting_population": None,
                              "measured": False}
        else:
            per_host[host] = {
                "over_threshold": sum(1 for r in over if r["host"] == host),
                "waiting_population": sum(1 for r in rows if r["host"] == host),
                "measured": True,
            }
    return {
        "over_threshold": len(over) if any_observed else None,
        "over_threshold_by_kind": by_kind if any_observed else None,
        "waiting_population": len(rows) if any_observed else None,
        "waiting_population_by_kind": pop_by_kind if any_observed else None,
        "waited_unmeasured": sum(1 for r in rows if r["waited_secs"] is None),
        "per_host": per_host,
        # 🔴 True whenever ANY scope went unmeasured. A consumer that renders
        # `over_threshold` without reading this is rendering a partial count as
        # a total.
        "counts_are_partial": not coverage["complete"],
    }


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Fetching the report
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def default_runner(argv, timeout=300):
    return subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)


def fetch_report(script_path, runner=default_runner, extra_argv=()):
    """Invoke `session-manager` and parse its JSON. Read-only: this is the only
    subprocess this tool ever launches, it mutates no tmux state, and no
    pattern is ever handed to a process-killing command."""
    argv = [sys.executable, script_path] + list(SESSION_MANAGER_ARGV) + list(extra_argv)
    proc = runner(argv)
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError("session-manager exited %s: %s"
                           % (proc.returncode, (proc.stderr or "").strip()[:400]))
    return json.loads(proc.stdout)


def read_report_file(path):
    if path == "-":
        return json.load(sys.stdin)
    with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        return json.load(fh)


def resolve_threshold(flag_value, environ):
    """-> (secs, source). Flag beats env beats default, and the SOURCE is
    published so a tuned run is never mistaken for a default one."""
    if flag_value is not None:
        return flag_value, "flag"
    raw = environ.get(THRESHOLD_ENV)
    if raw not in (None, ""):
        try:
            val = int(raw)
        except ValueError:
            return DEFAULT_WAIT_THRESHOLD, "default (env %s not an integer: %r)" % (
                THRESHOLD_ENV, raw)
        if val <= 0:
            return DEFAULT_WAIT_THRESHOLD, "default (env %s not positive: %r)" % (
                THRESHOLD_ENV, raw)
        return val, "env"
    return DEFAULT_WAIT_THRESHOLD, "default"


def humanize(secs):
    if secs is None:
        return "unmeasured"
    secs = int(secs)
    if secs < 60:
        return "%ds" % secs
    if secs < 3600:
        return "%dm" % (secs // 60)
    if secs < 86400:
        return "%dh%02dm" % (secs // 3600, (secs % 3600) // 60)
    return "%dd%02dh" % (secs // 86400, (secs % 86400) // 3600)


def apply_top(rows, top):
    """🔴 NO SILENT CAPS. Returns (shown, truncation) where `truncation` is
    `None` only when nothing was dropped; otherwise it names the counts, and
    every renderer prints it."""
    if top is None or top <= 0 or len(rows) <= top:
        return rows, None
    return rows[:top], {"shown": top, "dropped": len(rows) - top,
                        "total": len(rows)}


def build_report(report, stamps, now, threshold_secs, threshold_source,
                 state_status, state_file, top=None):
    core, next_stamps = reconcile(report, stamps, now, threshold_secs)
    over = [r for r in core["rows"] if r["over_threshold"]]
    shown, truncation = apply_top(over, top)
    out = {
        "tool": "waiting-windows",
        "now": now,
        "source_ts": report.get("ts"),
        "threshold_secs": threshold_secs,
        "threshold_source": threshold_source,
        "counts": core["counts"],
        "coverage": core["coverage"],
        "over_threshold": shown,
        "waiting_population": core["rows"],
        "truncated": truncation,
        "state": {
            "path": state_file,
            "status": state_status,
            "stamps_total": len(next_stamps),
            "prune": core["prune"],
        },
        "caveats": {
            "idle_no_signal": IDLE_NO_SIGNAL_CAVEAT,
            "clock": CLOCK_CAVEAT,
            "generation": GENERATION_CAVEAT,
        },
    }
    return out, next_stamps


def render_human(out):
    L = []
    thr = out["threshold_secs"]
    L.append("waiting-windows — threshold %s (%ss, %s)"
             % (humanize(thr), thr, out["threshold_source"]))
    counts = out["counts"]
    total = counts["over_threshold"]
    if total is None:
        L.append("OVER THRESHOLD: UNMEASURED — no host was observed this run.")
    else:
        label = "OVER THRESHOLD: %d" % total
        if counts["counts_are_partial"]:
            label += "  ⚠ PARTIAL — some scopes were not measured (see COVERAGE)"
        L.append(label)
        bk = counts["over_threshold_by_kind"] or {}
        L.append("  by kind: " + ", ".join("%s=%d" % (k, bk.get(k, 0))
                                           for k in ALL_KINDS))
    L.append("")
    rows = out["over_threshold"]
    if not rows:
        L.append("  (nothing over the threshold in the measured scope)")
    for r in rows:
        L.append("  %-9s %-18s %s:%s (%s%s)"
                 % (humanize(r["waited_secs"]), r["kind"],
                    r["session"], r["window_index"], r["window_id"],
                    "" if r["generation_checked"] else " gen-UNCHECKED"))
        L.append("      %s%s  [waited_source=%s, age_secs=%s]"
                 % (r["label"] or "?",
                    " (%s)" % r["hotkey"] if r["hotkey"] else "",
                    r["waited_source"],
                    "null" if r["age_secs"] is None else int(r["age_secs"])))
        if r["task"]:
            L.append("      task: %s" % r["task"])
    if out["truncated"]:
        t = out["truncated"]
        L.append("")
        L.append("  ⚠ TRUNCATED: showing %d of %d; %d NOT shown."
                 % (t["shown"], t["total"], t["dropped"]))
    L.append("")
    cov = out["coverage"]
    L.append("COVERAGE: %s" % ("complete" if cov["complete"]
                               else "INCOMPLETE — counts above are partial"))
    if cov["hosts_unobserved"]:
        L.append("  hosts NOT observed (their counts are null, not 0): %s"
                 % ", ".join(cov["hosts_unobserved"]))
    if cov["hosts_captures_unmeasured"]:
        L.append("  hosts whose pane captures were NOT measured: %s"
                 % ", ".join(cov["hosts_captures_unmeasured"]))
    if cov["generation_unchecked_hosts"]:
        L.append("  hosts with NO tmux server pid — generation UNCHECKED: %s"
                 % ", ".join(cov["generation_unchecked_hosts"]))
    if cov["rows_unmeasured"]:
        L.append("  rows whose waiting signal was not measured: %d (%s)"
                 % (cov["rows_unmeasured"],
                    ", ".join("%s=%d" % kv for kv in
                              sorted(cov["rows_unmeasured_reasons"].items()))))
    if cov["rows_without_window_id"]:
        L.append("  rows with no window_id, so no clock could be kept: %d"
                 % cov["rows_without_window_id"])
    if cov["windows_unmeasured"]:
        L.append("  windows unmeasured upstream: %d" % len(cov["windows_unmeasured"]))
    L.append("")
    L.append("CAVEAT (idle_no_signal): %s" % IDLE_NO_SIGNAL_CAVEAT)
    L.append("CAVEAT (clock): %s" % CLOCK_CAVEAT)
    return "\n".join(L)


def main(argv=None, environ=None, now=None, state_file=None):
    environ = os.environ if environ is None else environ
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="waiting-windows",
        description="Windows that have been waiting on the operator longer than "
                    "a threshold, oldest first. Read-only. Consumes "
                    "session-manager's waiting predicate; never re-derives it.")
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="JSON output")
    p.add_argument("--json-file", metavar="PATH",
                   help="read the session-manager report from PATH ('-' for "
                        "stdin) instead of invoking session-manager")
    p.add_argument("--threshold-secs", type=int, default=None,
                   help="override the threshold (default %d = %s; env %s)"
                        % (DEFAULT_WAIT_THRESHOLD,
                           humanize(DEFAULT_WAIT_THRESHOLD), THRESHOLD_ENV))
    p.add_argument("--top", type=int, default=None,
                   help="show at most N rows. What is dropped is ALWAYS printed.")
    p.add_argument("--state-file", default=None, help="override the state file path")
    p.add_argument("--no-state", action="store_true",
                   help="do not read or write the state file. Every row then "
                        "reports waited_source=ledger_age_fallback.")
    args = p.parse_args(argv)

    threshold, source = resolve_threshold(args.threshold_secs, environ)
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    path = state_file or args.state_file or state_path()

    here = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
    if args.json_file:
        report = read_report_file(args.json_file)
    else:
        report = fetch_report(os.path.join(here, "session-manager"))

    if args.no_state:
        loaded = {"stamps": {}, "status": "disabled"}
    else:
        loaded = load_state(path)

    out, next_stamps = build_report(report, loaded["stamps"], now, threshold,
                                    source, loaded["status"], path, top=args.top)
    if not args.no_state:
        save_state(path, next_stamps)

    if args.json:
        json.dump(out, sys.stdout, indent=1, sort_keys=True)
        sys.stdout.write("\n")
    else:
        sys.stdout.write(render_human(out) + "\n")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
